因为当然，当他们在南方组织所有那些游行时，他们心里有数，他们做好了会被攻击的心理准备，做好了被警察以及警察带着警犬等等非人对待的心理准备。他们会进监狱，经受这一切。他们会公开受难。非暴力，在别人攻击你时公开承受苦难，是他们战略中非常重要的一部分。我的意思是，马丁·路德·金显然，正如你所说，他是一位牧师。

23
00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:37.000
He is aware of the biblical echoes of that. The innocents who are persecuted by the apparatus of a great power. We know what the model is there. But what you have from about 1966-67 is not black people as suffering as the people who are being subjected to violence. But in the newspapers now you have stories about African Americans who are carrying out violence. So most obviously the rioters in the cities in 66, 67, but also you have younger activists now who say we have suffered enough. You know, the time for just meekly
他意识到了其中的圣经回响。那些被一个伟大机器的机构所迫害的无辜者。我们知道那里的原型是什么。但从1966到1967年前后，情况不再是黑人作为遭受暴力的受害者受苦受难，而是在报纸上，你开始看到关于非裔美国人实施暴力的报道。最明显的莫过于66年、67年城市里的暴动者，但同时你也有了更年轻的激进分子，他们说我们受够了。你知道，温顺游行的时代已经结束了。因此马尔科姆·X……

24
00:12:37.000 --> 00:13:00.000
marching is over. And so Malcolm X. Well, Malcolm X has already been shot at this point, but Malcolm X is of course a trailblazer, a harbinger for this kind of thing. The Black Power slogan comes from a guy called Stokely Carmichael, a much younger activist. He is on a march through the South in the summer of 1966 and he starts chanting Black Power, Black Power. And younger, more militant activists are like at last.
好吧，马尔科姆·X此时已经遇刺身亡了，但马尔科姆·X当然是这方面的先驱和先兆。黑人权力（Black Power）这个口号来自于一个叫斯托克利·卡迈克尔（Stokely Carmichael）的人，他是一个年轻得多的激进分子。他在1966年夏天穿越南方的游行中开始高呼“黑人权力，黑人权力”。更年轻、更激进的激进分子则觉得：终于来了。

25
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:23.000
There's a generational issue here. King is an old man to them. I mean, he's not old to us, but he's old to them. And they say, finally, you know, somebody who's got a bit of backbone will stand up to the white establishment. And also they're quite cool, aren't they? The Black Panther. Well, this is the thing. I mean, there's all the Tom Wolf radical chic stuff. The white liberals as well, you know, they're quite edgy and yeah, bring them along to dinner parties and things.
这里存在一个代际问题。对他们来说，金是个老头子。我的意思是，对我们来说他不老，但对他们来说他老了。他们说，终于有个有点骨气的人站出来对抗白人建制派了。而且他们还挺酷的，不是吗？黑豹党。没错，就是这样。我的意思是，所有那些汤姆·沃尔夫式的“激进时髦”（radical chic）的东西。白人自由派也是，你知道的，他们挺有前卫感的，对，把他们带到晚宴上之类的地方。

26
00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:58.000
Exactly. Exactly. So the Black Panthers really exposed onto the scene in the spring of 1967. People like Bobby Seale and Huey Newton, they have an armed invasion of the state capital in California, Sacramento. Huey Newton said, and I quote, political power comes to the barrel of a gun. We do not believe in passive and nonviolent tactics. They haven't worked for us black people. They are bankrupt. And do you think that, I mean, in the sixties, things are cool and you have to constantly be moving. You need new things by definition. If you stay with the old tried ways of doing things, you know, you're basically Cliff Richard. Yeah, you're square.
没错，没错。所以黑豹党在1967年春天真正轰动登场。像鲍比·希尔（Bobby Seale）和休伊·牛顿（Huey Newton）这样的人，他们在加州首府萨克拉门托对州议会大厦进行了武装突袭。休伊·牛顿说，我引用原话：“政治权力是从枪杆子里取得的。我们不相信消极和非暴力的策略。它们对我们黑人不管用。它们已经破产了。”你认为——我的意思是，在60年代，追求酷是很重要的，你必须不断前进。按定义你需要新鲜事物。如果你固守老一套的做事方式，你知道的，你基本上就是克里夫·理查德（Cliff Richard）。是的，你是个老古板。

27
00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:29.000
You're square. Yeah. I mean, is that, is that a kind of part of the climate as well? I think it is. I absolutely think it is. So the student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee, SNCC as it is called, which has been such a big part of the civil rights movement in the South. The name is the Nonviolent Coordinating Committee. By 1967, its new chairman, who is a man called H.Rap Brown, he says, everybody should arm themselves and quote wage guerrilla war on the honky white man. Let's make the Viet Cong look like Sunday school teachers.
老古板。是的。我的意思是，这也是当时社会风气的一部分吗？我认为是的。我绝对认为是的。因此，学生非暴力协调委员会（SNCC），也就是人们所说的这个曾在南方的民权运动中发挥过如此巨大作用的组织。它的名字叫“非暴力协调委员会”。到了1967年，它的新主席，一个叫H·拉普·布朗（H. Rap Brown）的人，他说，每个人都应该武装自己，我引用原话：“对白鬼子发动游击战。我们要让越共看起来像主日学校老师一样温和。”

28
00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:54.000
Groovy. I mean, this is exactly the kind of thing that seizes people's imagination. It is fashionable, frankly, in the late 1960s and Martin Luther King is regarded as yesterday's man. Have you ever written for the New York review of books, Tom? I haven't, no. Well, just as well, because they shame themselves at this point. They publish an article about Martin Luther King by a guy called Andrew Copkin, August 1967. King once had the ability
太时髦了。我的意思是，这正是那种能抓住人们想象力的东西。坦率地说，这在20世纪60年代末是非常时髦的，而马丁·路德·金则被视为过气人物。你给《纽约书评》写过稿子吗，汤姆？我没有，没有。好吧，这也罢，因为他们这时候真是在自取其辱。他们在1967年8月发表了一篇由安德鲁·科普金（Andrew Copkin）撰写的关于马丁·路德·金的文章，文中写道：“金曾经有能力

29
00:14:54.000 --> 00:15:16.000
to talk to people, the power to change them. But the duty of a revolutionary is to make revolutions and King has made none. He has been outstripped by his times, overtaken by events which he may have obliquely helped to produce but could not predict. He has been outstripped by his times, overtaken by events which he may have obliquely helped to produce but could not predict. He's not likely to regain command." Kind of goodbye granddad. Right. I mean, it's kind of like, you know, this guy hasn't kept up with Sergeant Pepper or whatever. Exactly.
与人们对话，有改变他们的力量。但革命者的职责是发动革命，而金没有发动任何革命。他被他的时代抛弃了，被那些他可能间接推波助澜却无法预测的事件所超越。他被他的时代抛弃了，被那些他可能间接推波助澜却无法预测的事件所超越。他不太可能重新掌权。”有点像是“再见吧，爷爷”。没错。我的意思是，这有点像……你知道的，这家伙没有跟上《佩珀军士》（Sergeant Pepper）或者诸如此类的潮流。没错。

30
00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:37.000
It's almost the same thing. You know, you've got to keep up with the vibe. Yeah. The fact that he has been outpaced by his times, that he hasn't produced results, black Americans are still likely to be poor, they're still likely to go to prison, all of this stuff. The time that nonviolence is over, the time that urgency has begun. That's basically what people are saying. And is Andrew Copkin black or white? He's white.
几乎是同一回事。你知道的，你得跟上时代的浪潮。是的。他被时代超越了，他没有产生结果，美国黑人依然很可能贫困，依然很可能进监狱，所有这些事情。非暴力运动的时代结束了，充满紧迫感的时代已经开始。这基本上就是人们在说的。而且安德鲁·科普金是黑人还是白人？他是白人。

31
00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:56.000
He's a white radical journalist. Right. Okay. I mean, he's writing a bit like a white radical has to be said. He is indeed. He is. So King feels this pressure very, very deeply. If you think he has for more than a decade, he has been in the public eye. He's been long days.
他是一个白人激进记者。好。我的意思是，他写起东西来的确有点白人激进分子的腔调。他确实如此。确实如此。所以金非常、非常深刻地感受到了这种压力。如果你想想看，他已经进入公众视野十多年了。日子很长。

32
00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:18.000
He's exhausted. He's been in prison 18 times. He has been punched in the face. He has been stabbed. He's been hit on the head with rocks. He's been tear gassed, sprayed with water cannons, had dogs set on him. All the time he is traveling, he's staying in kind of cheap hotels. This is a chilling precedent for us going on our tour of the United States, Tom.
他精疲力竭。他曾18次入狱。他的脸挨过拳头。他被刺伤过。他的头部被石头砸过。他中过催泪瓦斯，被水枪喷射过，被警犬撕咬过。他一直在旅行，住在各种廉价酒店里。这对我们去美国巡回演讲来说是一个令人不寒而栗的前车之鉴啊，汤姆。

33
00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:47.000
I hope this doesn't happen to us, staying in cheap hotels. They're not quite so saintly. And I look at this, he's smoking and drinking too much, gaining weight, taking sleeping pills, constantly traveling, speaking and preaching. This is like you on tour, frankly. Yeah it is. Anyway, he's under enormous stress. His marriage is also crumbling by the way, because one of the things that people I guess now know about Martin Luther King is that he is a man, he's ravaged by guilt because he's not perfect. He's a sinner.
我希望我们不会遭遇这种事，住在廉价酒店里。他们可没那么圣洁。我看着他，他烟酒过度、体重增加、靠安眠药度日，不断地旅行、演讲和布道。坦率地说，这简直就像你在巡演一样。确实是这样。不管怎样，他承受着巨大的压力。顺便说一句，他的婚姻也正在崩溃，因为人们现在对马丁·路德·金的了解之一就是，他是一个饱受内疚折磨的人，因为他不完美。他是个罪人。

34
00:16:47.000 --> 00:17:24.000
Yeah, he's a sinner. He has relationships outside his marriage. He confessed an affair to Coretta, his wife, over Christmas 1967. She was devastated, but she wasn't surprised. And she was, one of the things that devastated her was that actually he was in such a mess about it. He seemed, and I quote, terribly distressed. I had not seen him like this before. And also isn't he being hounded by the FBI and J. Edgar Hoover, who was kind of hoovering up scandalous details for his files and things like that. Yes. The FBI have had King under surveillance for years. J. Edgar Hoover tells LBJ, tells President Johnson in the course of 1967, that he's now convinced that King is quote, an
是的，他是个罪人。他在婚姻之外有婚外情。他在1967年圣诞节期间向妻子科雷塔坦白了一段婚外情。她崩溃了，但她并不感到惊讶。让她感到崩溃的原因之一实际上是，他对此陷入了极度混乱。他看起来——我引用原话——“极其痛苦。我以前没见过他这样。”而且难道他没有一直遭到联邦调查局（FBI）和J·埃德加·胡佛的纠缠吗？胡佛一直在为他的档案搜集丑闻细节等等。是的。联邦调查局对金进行多年的监视。J·埃德加·胡佛在1967年期间告诉LBJ（总统约翰逊），他现在确信金——我引用原话——是一个

35
00:17:24.000 --> 00:18:06.000
instrument in the hands of subversive forces seeking to undermine our nation. He is absolutely obsessed with King's love life. A Tomcat with obsessive degenerative sexual urges, Jay Gohoover says of King. He has him taped with one of his lovers in Washington Hotel. The FBI then sends that tape to King's wife anonymously for her to listen to. So yes, he knows, I mean, King is not an idiot. He knows that the authorities are watching him the whole time, spying on him, spreading rumors about him to the newspapers. He is under immense, immense moral psychological pressure and his reaction to
企图颠覆我们国家的颠覆势力手中的工具。他对金的私生活有着绝对的痴迷。“一个有着强迫性退化性性冲动的花花公子，”J·埃德加·胡佛这样评价金。他在华盛顿的一家酒店里录下了金和他的一个情妇在一起的磁带。然后联邦调查局匿名将这卷磁带寄给金的妻子，让她听。所以是的，他知道——我的意思是，金不是傻瓜。他知道当局一直在监视他、窥探他，向报纸散布关于他的谣言。他承受着巨大无比的道德、心理压力，而他对

36
00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:52.000
this actually, a lot of people say, okay, come on, as it were, wind your neck in, stop speaking out about these things, you know, go back to being the cuddly more moderate sort of moral prophet of talking about the South. But you use the word prophet and early you called him a secular saint. I mean, he is both, but ultimately he's a prophet in the biblical sense. He is inspired by the example of the biblical prophets who denounce injustice, but are themselves sinners. And that is, I mean, that's clearly what motivates him. It's what keeps him on the, on the trail, that sense of following in the line of Isaiah or Jeremiah or whatever. But it's also what gives him his enduring power. I mean, he is drawing on the rhythms and cadences
这一切的反应实际上是——许多人说：“行了吧，算了吧，收敛一点，别再谈论这些事情了，回到过去那种惹人喜爱、温和派的道德先知形象上去，去谈论南方吧。”但你用了“先知”这个词，早些时候你称他为“世俗圣人”。我的意思是，他两者皆是，但归根结底，他是《圣经》意义上的先知。他受到《圣经》先知们的榜样的启发，这些先知谴责不义，但他们自己也是罪人。这就是——我的意思是，这显然是激励他的动力。正是这种追随以赛亚或耶利米等人足迹的感觉，支撑着他继续在布道之路上前行。但这也是赋予他持久力量的源泉。我的意思是，他汲取了《圣经》先知们的韵律和

37
00:18:52.000 --> 00:19:24.000
of the biblical prophets. And that is the journey to which he feels he is summoned. Yes, exactly so. And that is why he does not tack back towards the center. He feels that he has literally a God given duty to keep going. So he starts saying to people, you know, we need to go further. We need to have an, I quote, a revolution of values, complete moral surgery, radical redistribution of economic power. Of course, this is why the FBI are calling him a communist. He's not a communist. He's a biblical prophet.
节奏。这就是他感到自己受召召唤踏上的旅程。是的，完全如此。这就是为什么他没有向中间路线妥协。他觉得上帝赋予了他必须坚持下去的绝对职责。所以他开始对人们说，你知道的，我们需要走得更远。我们需要进行——我引用原话——“价值观的革命”、彻底的道德手术、对经济权力的彻底重新分配。当然，这就是为什么联邦调查局称他为共产党人。他不是共产主义者。他是一个《圣经》先知的化身。

38
00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:44.000
Yeah. But he's also his politics are those of, you know, a sort of Western European democratic socialist. You know, what he's talking about is- I mean, practically that's the effect. Of course, the religious stuff, I totally agree with you. I mean, he is nothing if not a religious figure, Martin Luther King. He comes from the church. He's steeped in the church and in the values that his father, also a
是的。但他的政治立场也是那种……你知道的，某种西欧民主社会主义者的立场。他所谈论的是——从实际上来看，这就是效果。当然，宗教方面的内容，我完全同意你的看法。我的意思是，马丁·路德·金如果不是一个宗教人物的话，就什么都不是了。他出身于教会。他深深沉浸在教会以及他的父亲——同样是一位

39
00:19:44.000 --> 00:20:20.000
preacher, had instilled in him. And partly because of this prophet thing, and again, to completely echo what you've been saying, at the end of 1967, he unveils his latest campaign, which is very kind of biblical. He says, I want to force America to confront the reality of poverty. And I want to have a massive demonstration in the heart of the capital of Washington, DC, it's going to be called the Poor People's Campaign. They will build a shanty town in the heart of Washington called Resurrection City, so it'll be in sight of the halls of the rich and powerful.
牧师——灌输给他的价值观念之中。部分由于这种“先知”的使命感，再次完全呼应你刚才所说的话，在1967年底，他推出了他的最新竞选活动，这非常有《圣经》色彩。他说：“我要迫使美国正视贫困的现实。我希望在华盛顿特区市中心发动一场大规模的示威活动，它将被称为‘穷人运动’。”他们将在华盛顿市中心建造一个名为“复活城”的棚户区，这样它就能进入那些富裕和权贵大厅的视野。

40
00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:34.000
And he says we will have African-Americans, we will have native Americans, they're Indians, we'll have poor whites from Appalachia, we will have African-Americans, we will have native Americans, they're Indians. We'll have poor whites from Appalachia. We'll have Puerto Ricans. We'll have Pacific Islanders. We will have Eskimos. Have the works. Yeah.
他说，我们将有非裔美国人，我们将有美洲原住民——也就是印第安人，我们将有来自阿巴拉契亚的贫穷白人，我们将有非裔美国人，我们将有美洲原住民，也就是印第安人。我们将有来自阿巴拉契亚的贫穷白人。我们将有波多黎各人。我们将有太平洋岛民。我们将有爱斯基摩人。应有尽有。对。

41
00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:54.000
We will have the most marginalized people in the United States and we will bring them to the Capitol. So it's Lazarus at the gates of Deves. It is. Now, the thing is, lots of people in it, as allies say, this is a really bad idea. What will happen if it all goes wrong? What will happen if there's violence? We will get the blame, you will get the blame and you will damage our cause.
我们将动员美国最被边缘化的人群，把他们带到国会山。所以这就是拉撒路站在富人门前的翻版。确实如此。问题是，里面的许多人，比如助手们都说，这是一个非常糟糕的主意。如果一切搞砸了会怎么样？如果发生暴力事件会怎么样？我们会受到指责，你会受到指责，并且你会损害我们的事业。

42
00:20:54.000 --> 00:21:15.000
The establishments are very different from the Martyr in Washington in 1963. The establishments of course have been very suspicious of that as well, but Kennedy and Co. eventually grudgingly come round to it. It's the first time Kennedy watches Martin Luther King speak, isn't it, at that? Yeah. And says, my God, he's good. But this time it is clear the authorities will not cooperate at all.
当时的建制派与1963年华盛顿大游行时的建制派非常不同。当然，当时的建制派对那次游行也非常怀疑，但肯尼迪及其幕僚最终勉强接受了。那是肯尼迪第一次看马丁·路德·金演讲，对吧？是的。并且说：“天哪，他讲得真好。”但这一次，很显然，当局根本不会配合。

43
00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:50.000
In fact, the FBI are making plans to undermine it. And so as 1968 begins, King is all devoted to this poor people's campaign, but his aides are very worried you know, worried about it. He's under all this pressure from the authorities about it. And then he gets an invitation to come to Memphis, Tennessee. So Memphis has not really featured in the civil rights story to this point. It's on the banks of the Mississippi. It's a tough town, Memphis. It had once been one of the biggest slave markets in the South run by the founder of the Ku Klux Klan, General Nathan Bedford Forrest.
事实上，联邦调查局正在制定计划来破坏它。因此，随着1968年的开始，金把所有的精力都投入到了这场“穷人运动”中，但他的助手们非常担心，你知道的，对此忧心忡忡。他正承受着来自当局的巨大压力。就在这时，他收到了前往田纳西州孟菲斯的邀请。因此，孟菲斯此前并未真正出现在民权故事中。它位于密西西比河畔。孟菲斯是一个艰难的城市。它曾是南方最大的奴隶市场之一，由三K党创始人内森·贝德福德·福雷斯特将军经营。

44
00:21:50.000 --> 00:22:12.000
Memphis had become a magnet for migrants from the countryside in the early 20th century, this is where you get the blues. Yeah. So Beale Street, the home of the blues in Memphis, all these clubs. But most people of course were not blues musicians. They are janitors. They are waiters, they're cooks. They're very poorly treated, poorly paid. And maids.
20世纪初，孟菲斯成为了吸引乡村移民的磁石，这也是布鲁斯音乐（蓝调）的诞生地。是的。所以比尔街（Beale Street）——孟菲斯的蓝调发源地，所有这些俱乐部。但大多数人当然不是蓝调音乐家。他们是门房。他们是服务员、厨师。他们受到极坏的对待，薪水极低。还有女佣。

45
